{
  "id": "addresses-on-demand",
  "name": "Addresses on Demand",
  "category": "services",
  "difficulty": 2,
  "xp": 250,
  "level": 7,
  "description": "Stop typing addresses by hand. Let DHCP do it.",
  "brief": "PC-02 has no address at all, and DHCP-01 has nothing to hand out.\n\nConfigure a pool on DHCP-01 covering 10.0.1.100 to 10.0.1.110, with mask 255.255.255.0, gateway 10.0.1.1 and DNS 10.0.2.53. Then set PC-02 to obtain its address automatically and run 'dhcp renew' in its terminal.\n\nWatch the four steps go past in the event log: DISCOVER, OFFER, REQUEST, ACK.",
  "hints": [
    "Select DHCP-01. It is already listening on 67/udp — it just needs a pool.",
    "The pool's gateway and DNS are what the clients will be told to use.",
    "On PC-02, tick 'Obtain an address automatically'.",
    "Then run 'dhcp renew' on PC-02 and check 'ipconfig /all'."
  ],
  "explanation": "DHCP is a four-message conversation: the client shouts DISCOVER because it has no address yet, a server OFFERs one, the client REQUESTs that specific offer, and the server ACKs it.\n\nThe important part is what the lease carries. It is not just an address — the mask, gateway and DNS server all come from the pool, which means a mistake in the pool becomes a mistake on every client that asks.",
